WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.090
As a young stand-up comedian, Adam Sandler
probably didn't foresee himself becoming one

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:09.180
of the most successful funnymen in Hollywood,
complete with a sweet Netflix deal, but he

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.330
did. While his movies are often beaten up
on by critics, the actor has starred in some

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:17.099
pretty classic comedies, like Happy Gilmore,
Billy Madison, and The Wedding Singer.

00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:20.359
"Son of a b---- ball why didn't you just go
home."

00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:24.420
However, if you look long enough at the man's
movies, you'll spot numerous in-jokes and

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.919
all sorts of references scattered throughout
his filmography.

00:00:26.919 --> 00:00:28.409
The Star Wars connection

00:00:28.409 --> 00:00:31.909
There's no denying Sandler's films have a
pretty strong connection to Star Wars.

00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:39.550
"Millennium Falcon, Millennium Falcon. Cruising
along here. Whooo, almost hit a star."

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:43.050
Take Carrie Fisher, for example. The late
great actress is best remembered for her role

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.210
as Princess Leia, but did you know she also
worked on The Wedding Singer? As it turns

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:51.969
out, Fisher was an uncredited writer on the
1998 comedy, and according to Drew Barrymore,

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:55.239
Fisher was brought in to "write the girl's
part to make it balanced."

00:00:55.239 --> 00:01:00.269
But the connections don't stop there. In 2012's
That's My Boy, Sandler plays Donny Berger,

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:02.819
a deadbeat dad who names his kid "Han Solo."

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:04.830
"You were basically the worst parent ever."

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.150
"I was awesome!"

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.610
Sandler's loyalty to this galaxy far, far
away might stem from a real-life interaction

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:15.850
he had with Han. According to Sandler, Harrison
Ford once asked him to wash his car as a way

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.280
to surprise his kids, as they were big Sandler
fans.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.759
"He goes, my kids think you're funny. And
it would just be such a kick for them to see

00:01:24.759 --> 00:01:27.590
that. And I was like, 'Uh, OK.'"

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:32.100
And if the galaxy's greatest space scoundrel
asks you for a favor, how can you say no?

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.430
The Godfather connection

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:38.060
Adam Sandler's extended movie universe doesn't
stop at Star Wars. The actor is also a big

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.280
fan of The Godfather, and a few of his films
contain references to Francis Ford Coppola's

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:47.430
classic gangster series. For proof, look no
further than Jack and Jill, which stars Michael

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:52.270
Corleone himself. The actor even references
the infamous scene from The Godfather Part

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:56.579
II where he threatens Diane Keaton with all
his power, asking her, "Don't you know me?"

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:01.460
Of course, in Jack and Jill, Pacino uses the
monologue to intimidate Sandler into getting

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.200
him a date with his twin sister.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.289
And in Billy Madison, Sandler did his own
Michael Corleone impersonation, spoofing the

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:10.840
scene from The Godfather Part II where Michael
gives Fredo the kiss of death.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.860
A Vendetta for V

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.030
Everybody has a type, and it seems like Adam
Sandler has a thing for women whose names

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:22.220
start with "V." Or against them, maybe? In
Billy Madison, Bridgette Wilson plays Veronica

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:27.190
Vaughn, a third-grade teacher who has strange
ways of motivating Billy to take his education

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:31.650
more seriously. Then, in Happy Gilmore, Sandler's
character is encouraged to get his head in

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.090
the golfing game by a PR executive named Virginia
Venit.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:40.000
And in The Waterboy, Bobby Boucher falls in
love with witchy astrologer Vicki Vallencourt

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.950
, despite his mama's warnings.

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.210
"That woman is the devil, and I want you to
stay away from her, you hear me?"

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.210
"Yes, Mama."

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.450
"Now you come on inside, before that lil'
ol' witch cast a spell on ya!"

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:54.970
Big Daddy also saw its title slacker dumped
by his girlfriend Vanessa, and in Little Nicky,

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:59.470
Sandler's son of Satan falls head over heels
for Valerie Veran, a woman who helps Nicky

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:00.980
defeat his evil brothers.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:05.190
And now we're wondering if maybe Adam Sandler
was the guy behind that Guy Fawkes mask…

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:06.900
The Tarantino connection

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.230
While The Ridiculous 6 didn't fare particularly
well with critics, there's no denying the

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:15.720
title is an intentional nod to Quentin Tarantino's
The Hateful Eight, which came out the same

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:19.700
year. But believe it or not, Adam Sandler
almost had the opportunity to work with Tarantino

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:24.530
himself, as the director originally wanted
the comedian to play the bat-wielding "Bear

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.810
Jew" in Inglourious Basterds.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:30.870
Of course, the role eventually went to Eli
Roth, as Sandler chose to star in Funny People,

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:35.190
instead. But while Sandler missed out on a
great role, Sandler's pal Jonathan Loughran

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:40.060
managed to catch the Tarantino train. Loughran
has shown up in quite a few Sandler films,

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.370
but he's also taken serious roles with Tarantino,
like playing Jasper the mechanic in Death

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:49.090
Proof. He also appeared in Kill Bill as a
gross trucker, so perhaps it's not a coincidence

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.320
that when Loughran popped up in Sandler's
Sandy Wexler, he was playing a character by

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.870
the name of Trucker Jon.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.300
The cat connection

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.550
There's a feline theme running through a lot
of Adam Sandler's movies, too. Anger Management,

00:04:00.550 --> 00:04:06.330
for example, was a film where Sandler's character
designs clothing for his overweight pet, Meatball.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:09.840
(Interestingly, that's the name of Sandler's
real-life dog, who served as the ring bearer

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.160
at Sandler's wedding.) Then there's Mr. Deeds,
where Sandler is slandered by a dishonest

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.709
reporter after he tried to save some cats
from a fire by tossing them out of a window.

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:24.069
In Don't Mess with the Zohan, Sandler's character
actually plays hacky sack with some poor cat,

00:04:24.069 --> 00:04:28.570
and in Funny People, Sandler lets loose with
a few laughs as he watches a kid perform "Memory"

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.861
from, you guessed it, Cats. But hey, we're
not done with the feline jokes just yet. In

00:04:32.861 --> 00:04:37.319
I Now Pronounce You Chuck and Larry, Chuck
falls for Alex when she's dressed up as a

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:41.060
Cat woman for Halloween. Meow there's a thread
worth tugging at.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:45.229
His love-hate relationship with felines probably
has a little something to do with that time

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:48.449
he was mauled by a cheetah during an African
safari, but who knows.

00:04:48.449 --> 00:04:51.880
Thanks for watching! Click the Looper icon
to subscribe to our YouTube channel. Plus

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.890
check out all this cool stuff we know you'll
love, too!

